# The attack, in two lines. Runs under BOTH configs and is not config-aware.
#
# The agent reads a page that arrived from outside the trust boundary and does what
# the page tells it to. Under rung 1's settings that succeeds, and correctly so:
# write_config is in task C's manifest, the arguments are well-formed, and no rule
# anywhere is broken. Under --ladder-taint the same two lines are refused, and the
# only thing that changed is where the bytes came from.
#
# --obey-instructions is the injection stand-in. It parses the LADDER-INSTRUCTION
# line out of the page and executes it, so "untrusted input steered the agent" is a
# mechanical fact and not an assumption about a model.

read-source /untrusted/injected-page.txt --obey-instructions

# The legitimate half of the same task, attempted after the injected action. It is a
# read-only tool and it is in scope -- but the sandbox is tainted by now, so rung 2
# refuses it too. Tainting is per-sandbox, not per-tool: this line is where the demo
# shows the cost of that honestly rather than picking a flattering example.
call-broker --tag after-injection read_wiki deploy-runbook
